<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mashonaland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mashonaland is the region of northern and north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> inhabited mainly by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a> people. Claimed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> kingdom, it was occupied in 1890 by the Pioneer Column of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a>, which founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harare">Fort Salisbury</a>, later Harare. Its people rose against company rule in 1896–1897, and today it forms three provinces of Zimbabwe.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mashonaland.jpg" alt="Mashonaland" width="320" height="292" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mangwanani using Inkscape · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mashonaland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mashonaland is a region in northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, home to nearly half of the country's population. Most of its people belong to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a> nation, and the Zezuru and Korekore dialects are the most common. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harare">Harare</a>, the capital and largest city, lies entirely within it and forms a province of its own; Chitungwiza is the next largest city. The territory is a broad plateau sloping gradually to the north and north-west. Its northern border is formed by the Zambezi River, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> beyond.</p><p>About half the land lies above 1,200 metres, and the central watershed in the south and centre is at 1,500 to 1,650 metres. Much of the landscape is rolling low hills divided by river valleys. The highest point is in the Wedza Mountains in the south-east, at 1,789 metres. Most of the region drains into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> basin, while a small part at the south-eastern edge drains to the Save River.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Mashonaland was originally one of the regions into which the country was divided after occupation by the Pioneer Column in 1890. It was the territory under the administration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a>, distinct from the land directly controlled by the Matabele king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lobengula">Lobengula</a>, which was named Matabeleland when it was occupied in 1893. The two had separate administrations for part of the company's colonial period.</p><p>A revolt against the company broke out in 1896, led by priests of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwari-cult">Mwari</a> religion. The British prevailed, executed some leaders and tried to reform the system. In 1923 the territory became part of the self-governing colony of Southern Rhodesia, and Mashonaland became one of its five provinces. An administrative reform in 1970 divided it into a northern and a southern half, and in 1983 it was divided into the present three provinces, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harare">Harare</a> given its own status. Since the constitutional amendments that took effect in 1988, each province is run by a governor appointed by the president.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mashonaland is divided into the provinces of Mashonaland West, Mashonaland Central and Mashonaland East, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harare">Harare</a>. About 7.4 million people live there, around half of the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>. The region has mineral resources including gold, platinum, nickel, chrome, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and lithium, and its main agricultural products include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, tobacco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, citrus and beef. Manufacturing is concentrated in and around Harare and Kadoma, and electricity comes from the Kariba South power station.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mashonaland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its Pioneer Column marched into <b>Mashonaland</b> and raised the flag at Fort Salisbury in September 1890.</blockquote><a href="/state/company-rule-in-rhodesia">Company rule in Rhodesia</a></li></ul></section>
